---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import AsciiTable from '../../components/AsciiTable.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="ASCII Table — Decimal, Hex & Character Reference" description="Searchable printable ASCII table — look up characters by decimal, hex, or the character itself.">
  <AsciiTable client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>ASCII to Hex Quick Reference</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Character</th><th>Decimal</th><th>Hex</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Space</td><td>32</td><td>0x20</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A</td><td>65</td><td>0x41</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Z</td><td>90</td><td>0x5A</td></tr>
      <tr><td>a</td><td>97</td><td>0x61</td></tr>
      <tr><td>z</td><td>122</td><td>0x7A</td></tr>
      <tr><td>0</td><td>48</td><td>0x30</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Newline</td><td>10</td><td>0x0A</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tab</td><td>9</td><td>0x09</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Use the searchable tool above to convert any character to its full decimal, hex, and binary representation instantly.</p>

  <h2>Why the ASCII table still matters</h2>
  <p>Despite Unicode's dominance, the first 128 characters of Unicode are identical to ASCII — and many low-level programming tasks (parsing, encoding, bit manipulation) still reference ASCII decimal or hex values directly.</p>

  <h2>Features</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Searchable by character, decimal value, or hex value</li>
    <li>Covers the full printable ASCII range</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Type a character, decimal number, or hex value into the search box.</li>
    <li>Matching entries are highlighted instantly.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>The uppercase letter <code>A</code> has decimal value <code>65</code> and hex value <code>0x41</code>.</p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Assuming all Unicode characters have ASCII values.</strong> Only the first 128 code points overlap — anything beyond that (emoji, accented characters, non-Latin scripts) is outside ASCII entirely.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing character codes with byte encoding.</strong> A character's ASCII value is straightforward, but how it's stored in memory (UTF-8, UTF-16) can differ, especially for non-ASCII characters.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use cases</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Looking up the decimal value of a control character (like newline or tab) for a parser</li>
    <li>Converting a character to its hex representation for a byte-level protocol</li>
    <li>Learning or teaching the basics of character encoding</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does this cover extended ASCII or Unicode?</strong> This reference covers standard 7-bit ASCII (0-127) — extended ASCII and Unicode code points beyond that range aren't included.</p>
</ToolLayout>
